    "questionText": "(I) In the 3rd and 2nd millennia BCE, Eurasian networks of commercial and information exchanges reached further than ever before. (II) By 2000 BCE, there existed trading cities in Central Asia that had contacts with Mesopotamia, northern India, and China, linking vast areas of Eurasia into loose networks of exchange. (III) Late in the first millennium BCE, goods and ideas began travelling regularly from the Mediterranean to China and vice versa along what came to be known as the Silk Roads. (IV) The appearance of agricultural technologies supported larger, denser, and more varied communities and created first urban civilisations. (V) The scale of these exchange networks may help explain the universalistic claims of religions of this era, such as Zoroastrianism, Buddhism, and Christianity.",
    "options": {
      "A": "I",
      "B": "II",
      "C": "III",
      "D": "IV",
      "E": "V"
    },
    "correctAnswer": "D",
